	<div class="page-section">
		<h2>How the best runners pace</h2>
		<p>Knowing the target pace is one thing. How you run to it is another.</p>
		<p>There is no single approach at the front of the field. Pick any of the runners below and you'll see completely different execution at the top five. The CS (consistency score) is a measure of how much a runner's loops vary from their own average. Lower means more predictable.</p>

		<h3>Running tight</h3>
		<p>Harvey Lewis ran Go One More 2026 with a CS of 3.6%. His loops clustered within about 45 seconds of his median across two days and nights. Jon Noll at Big's 2025 ran 111 laps at CS 4.2%. Find a pace, lock it in, hold it.</p>
		<p>For a crew, runners like this are the most manageable. You know almost exactly when they'll be back. Build a tight routine and you can run the tent like clockwork.</p>

		<h3>Banking time</h3>
		<p>Merijn Geerts ran Big's 2023 to a CS of 14.9%, with loops ranging from 46:45 to over 54:00. Phil Gore's spread at Big's 2025 was CS 24.0%, with loops as fast as 41 minutes and as slow as 52. That's not erratic running. Looking at the splits, both runners push fast laps early to bank extra tent time, then use it. Gore came back two years later and ran 14 more loops at Big's with the same approach.</p>
		<p>Crewing this style is harder in a different way. You can't plan around the clock. You plan around the runner.</p>

